## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design a hybrid cross-region analytics architecture that balances ultra-low latency for high-value (hot) tenants with cost-effective analytics for lower-value (cold) tenants. It tests knowledge of multi-tenant database patterns, caching strategies, cross-region replication behavior, and operational resilience.

## Key Concepts
- Aurora PostgreSQL Global Database with cross-region read replicas
- Row-Level Security (RLS) for tenant data isolation
- Read-through caching with Redis/ElastiCache
- CDC (Change Data Capture) for cache invalidation
- Amazon Redshift for cold-path analytics
- Cross-region replication lag monitoring and management
- IAM least privilege and KMS encryption
- DR/failover testing with AWS FIS and synthetic transactions

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tenant-scoped cache key generation
function buildCacheKey(tenantId, resourceType, resourceId) {
  return `${tenantId}:${resourceType}:${resourceId}`;
}

// WAL-based invalidation handler
async function handleWALEvent(event) {
  const { tenantId, resourceType, resourceId } = parseWALPayload(event);
  const cacheKey = buildCacheKey(tenantId, resourceType, resourceId);
  await redis.del(cacheKey);
  await publishInvalidationEvent(tenantId, cacheKey);
}

// Lag-aware read router
async function routeRead(ctx) {
  const lag = await getReplicationLag(ctx.tenantRegion);
  if (lag > MAX_ALLOWED_LAG) {
    throw new RetryableError('Replication lag exceeded');
  }
  return lag < CRITICAL_THRESHOLD 
    ? redis.getOrFetch(cacheKey, () => db.query(tenantId))
    : db.query(tenantId);
}
```

